WEBVTT

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:17.830
<v Ira Glass>We were putting together today's radio show, and we came across this idea that totally changed how we saw the stories that you're going to hear today. This idea was in a talk that George RR Martin gave. He's the guy who wrote the series of fantasy books that Game of Thrones was based on, back in the 1930s and '40s.
<v Ira Glass>我们在筹备今天的广播节目时，偶然发现了一个想法，它完全改变了我们对你们今天即将听到的故事的看法。这个想法来自乔治·R·R·马丁的一次演讲。他就是《权力的游戏》原著系列奇幻小说的作者。

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:37.140
<v Ira Glass>And he was talking about his job on stage in front of some fans, and specifically he was talking about how JRR Tolkien changed everything for people in his kind of work, for people who write fantasy stories. And when I say "changed everything," I don't mean putting a double R in the middle of your name if you're a fantasy writer. Tolkien, of course, wrote Lord of the Rings and The Hobbit .
<v Ira Glass>他在台上向粉丝讲述自己的工作，特别提到了J·R·R·托尔金是如何改变了他这一行——也就是奇幻小说创作者——的一切。当我提到“改变了一切”时，我并不是说如果是个奇幻作家，就得在名字中间加两个R。当然，托尔金创作了《指环王》和《霍比特人》。

00:00:37.140 --> 00:01:01.220
<v George Rr Martin>And one of the things he did that was extraordinary was create Middle Earth in such detail. If you look at some of the pre-Tolkien fantasy, it's written more in a story of fairy tales. You know, "Once upon a time, there was a king, and the king had a beautiful daughter." But you won't know, like, who was the king's father, or who was his grandfather, how the dynasty came to power, or what the neighboring countries are."
<v George Rr Martin>他做了一件非凡的事情，那就是创造了细节极其丰富的中土世界。如果你看看托尔金之前的奇幻作品，它们更像是在写童话故事。你知道的，比如“很久以前，有一个国王，国王有一个漂亮的女儿。”但你根本不知道国王的父亲是谁，祖父是谁，这个王朝是怎么掌权的，或者邻国是哪些。

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:09.910
<v Ira Glass>But Tolkien gave all that, and more. He created whole histories and languages and genealogies. He created stuff that never even appeared in the books.
<v Ira Glass>但托尔金提供了所有这些，甚至更多。他创造了完整的历史、语言和族谱。他甚至创造了一些从未在书中出现过的东西。

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:15.680
<v George Rr Martin>And it seemed as real as England or France or Germany when you read these things.
<v George Rr Martin>当你读到这些内容时，它看起来和英国、法国或德国一样真实。

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:37.470
<v Ira Glass>Make-believe just has more heft-- it gets to you more, it feels like more-- the more detail there is to make the world in the story feel real. Which when you think about it, of course, is obvious, whether you're telling a story, or you're designing Grand Theft Auto, or you're a spy needing to invent a cover, or you're a cheater or a con man of some kind, making up a scam.
<v Ira Glass>虚构的东西就是更有分量——它越能触动你，越让你感觉真实，是因为故事中的世界细节越丰富。当你仔细想想，这当然是很明显的，无论你是在讲故事，设计《侠盗猎车手》，还是需要伪造身份的间谍，又或者是某种行骗的骗子。

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:49.060
<v Ira Glass>But this idea was interesting to us, because today on our show, we have two stories of make-believe worlds. Act One is about an amazingly successful one. Act Two is about a fantastically unsuccessful one.
<v Ira Glass>但这个想法对我们来说很有趣，因为今天在我们的节目中，有两个关于虚构世界的故事。第一幕是关于一个极其成功的案例，第二幕是关于一个极其失败的案例。

00:01:49.060 --> 00:02:04.050
<v Ira Glass>And when we heard that quote from George RR Martin, we realized, right. These worlds live or die, their success or their failure depends, on these details, on how believable they are, how precisely chosen. That's at the heart of both these stories.
<v Ira Glass>当我们听到乔治·R·R·马丁那段话时，我们意识到，没错，这些世界的存亡，它们的成功或失败，都取决于这些细节，取决于它们的可信度以及细节的选择有多精准。这正是这两个故事的核心。

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:10.900
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. How make-believe works. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。了解虚构是如何运作的。请继续收听。

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:19.520
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐播放]

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:43.090
<v Ira Glass>Act One: Overboard. So there are 12 kids in the Steinfels family, and they grew up in the 1980s in a very unusual situation. They all worked on a ship. Their dad, Jim, had been in the Navy, and he oversaw them. They swabbed the decks. They served food in the chow line. They wore uniforms-- white sailor hats, black neckerchiefs. Miki Meek tells more.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：船上。斯坦费尔斯一家有12个孩子，他们在20世纪80年代成长于一种非常不同寻常的环境中。他们都在一艘船上工作。他们的父亲吉姆曾在海军服役，他监管着他们。他们擦洗甲板，在配餐线上分发食物。他们穿着制服——白色的水兵帽，黑色的领巾。米基·米克讲述了更多细节。

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:49.280
<v Miki Meek>When Joe was in elementary school, his favorite part of working on the ship was night watch. That's when all the rest of the crew was asleep.
<v Miki Meek>当乔还在读小学时，他最喜欢的工作是守夜。那是其他船员都在睡觉的时候。

00:02:49.280 --> 00:03:08.040
<v Joe>You know, which I loved, you know? I loved it. From midnight to 4:00 in the morning, that was one of my favorite watches, where it's quiet. You're listening to talk radio coming out with their little weather alerts and stuff. And you're sitting there, and every half-hour, you have to get up and walk around and make sure the ship's secure and all that.
<v Joe>你知道，我真的很喜欢，我太爱守夜了。从午夜到凌晨4点，那是我最喜欢的轮班之一，因为很安静。你会听着无线电里传来的天气预报之类的东西。你坐在那里，每半小时就要起来巡视一圈，确保船只安全之类的。

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.080
<v Radio Announcer>Colder as the evening and morning hours approach.
<v Radio Announcer>随着傍晚和清晨的临近，天气越来越冷。

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:17.970
<v Miki Meek>Joe was only six years old, little Navy hat on his head, peering over the side, watching for any incoming threat.
<v Miki Meek>乔当时只有六岁，头上戴着小小的海军帽，从船边探出头，观察是否有任何潜在的威胁。

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:24.330
<v Joe>I remember clearly, you know, there was raccoons one night that were over making noise near the garbage can area.
<v Joe>我记得很清楚，有一天晚上垃圾桶附近有浣熊在制造噪音。

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:40.490
<v Miki Meek>Yes. Raccoons. I should clarify. The ship that he worked on, it was not at sea. It was not moving. It was stationed in the driveway of their house in suburban Chicago. But Joe took his job seriously and kept a logbook that he updated almost every half-hour.
<v Miki Meek>没错。浣熊。我得澄清一下。他工作的这艘船并不在海上。它没有移动。它就停在芝加哥郊区他们家的车道上。但乔非常认真地对待他的工作，并保存了一份日志，几乎每半小时更新一次。

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:50.800
<v Joe>And so I think I annotated something along those lines in the logbook. You know, "2:30 in the morning, raccoons," such and such. So, "Made rounds. All is not secure. Raccoons."
<v Joe>所以我记得在日志里标注了类似的内容。你知道，“凌晨2点30分，浣熊”，诸如此类。“巡视完毕。一切不安全。有浣熊。”

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:57.280
<v Joe>You know, it gave so much officialness to what we were doing. This wasn't just a game. This was-- this was life.
<v Joe>你知道，这让我们做的事情显得非常正式。这不仅仅是一个游戏。这是——这是生活。

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:02.760
<v Joe>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Joe>[音乐播放]

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:24.520
<v Miki Meek>The idea to build a ship in the driveway came from his sister, Elizabeth. They shared a room, and one day when their parents told them to clean it up, Elizabeth made a deal with Joe. If he'd take care of it, she'd build them a ship. She was five; he was four. The big sister. Joe said yes. So Elizabeth dragged a couple boxes into their backyard and started to pull together materials.
<v Miki Meek>在车道上造船的想法来自他的姐姐伊丽莎白。他们共用一个房间，有一天父母让他们打扫房间时，伊丽莎白和乔达成了协议。如果乔负责打扫，她就为他们造一艘船。她五岁，他四岁。姐姐嘛。乔答应了。于是伊丽莎白拖了几个盒子到后院，开始收集材料。

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:49.450
<v Elizabeth>I think I asked my father if I could have some two-by-fours to make the deck with. And then, of course, he got involved, deciding that he was going to take it up a level. You know, signal flags being done up, and portholes in doors, and hand-painted numbers. And I had a gorgeous wood desk, and I had like my own phone, and landlines were run from the house to the ship.
<v Elizabeth>我想我问我父亲能不能给我一些2x4的木料来做甲板。当然，他参与了进来，决定把船升级一下。你知道，挂上信号旗，在门上开舷窗，还有手绘的编号。我有一个漂亮的木书桌，我还有自己的电话，家里还拉了固定电话到船上。

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:51.190
<v Miki Meek>What? Like a real phone?
<v Miki Meek>什么？像真电话一样？

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:52.100
<v Elizabeth>Yeah. Yeah.
<v Elizabeth>对，没错。

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:52.780
<v Miki Meek>What?
<v Miki Meek>什么？

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:55.320
<v Elizabeth>I'm telling you, I had a really nice office there.
<v Elizabeth>我告诉你，我那里的办公室真的很棒。

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.710
<v Miki Meek>And then did the ship have a name?
<v Miki Meek>那这艘船有名字吗？

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:03.210
<v Elizabeth>It was the USS Elizabeth City .
<v Elizabeth>它叫“伊丽莎白号”。

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:16.450
<v Miki Meek>As the months went on, the ship grew more and more elaborate. Their dad started using masonite and plywood. He put in a wheelhouse and an engine room. Eventually the ship was about 24 feet long and painted battleship gray.
<v Miki Meek>随着时间的推移，这艘船变得越来越精致。他们的父亲开始使用纤维板和胶合板。他安装了驾驶室和机舱。最终，这艘船大约24英尺长，漆成了战舰灰色。

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:27.100
<v Miki Meek>A lot of the magic was in the accessories. He outfitted the ship with guns that shot ping-pong balls and missiles made from spray-painted deodorant sticks. He picked up life jackets at a Navy surplus store.
<v Miki Meek>很多魔力来自于配件。他给船配备了可以发射乒乓球的枪，还有用喷漆除臭剂棒做的导弹。他从海军盈余商店买了救生衣。

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:33.360
<v Miki Meek>He installed an intercom. It was a long, hollow tube that let you talk from the wheelhouse to the engine room in the back.
<v Miki Meek>他安装了一个内部通讯系统。那是一个长长的中空管子，让你可以在驾驶室和后面的机舱之间说话。

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:43.370
<v Miki Meek>He attached the motor from a blender to a wall. Its purpose? Engine noise. The kids could hit different buttons speeds on it to make it sound like they were moving faster.
<v Miki Meek>他把一个搅拌机的马达装在墙上。它的用途是什么？模拟引擎噪音。孩子们可以按下不同的速度按钮，让声音听起来像是在加速行驶。

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:53.020
<v Miki Meek>What made their dad so good at make-believe is how real he made it. One of the girls, Marion, liked eating breakfast on the ship's deck. Her dad would bring out an old coffee percolator.
<v Miki Meek>让他们的父亲在虚构方面如此成功的原因在于他做得有多真实。其中一个女孩，玛丽恩，喜欢在船甲板上吃早餐。她父亲会拿出一个旧的咖啡过滤器。

00:05:53.020 --> 00:06:24.120
<v Marion>And put in the smokestack. And then you would just see the steam kind of rising up out of that. And then we had these little Kool-Aid mugs that had been remade into our coffee cups. And he really would let us have coffee. Like we would just sit around, like in our work gear, getting ready for the day and admiring our ship, with the real-looking steam coming up.
<v Marion>然后把它放进烟囱里。你会看到蒸汽从里面升起来。然后我们有这些小Kool-Aid马克杯，被改造成我们的咖啡杯。他真的会让我们喝咖啡。我们就坐在那里，穿着我们的工作服，准备开始新的一天，欣赏着我们的船，看着那看起来像真的一样的蒸汽升起。

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:44.840
<v Miki Meek>The kids would play on the USS Elizabeth City all summer long, sometimes on 24-hour shifts. Think of what genius parenting that is if you have a dozen kids. When summer comes, you want to keep them busy, engage them. In any given summer, pretty much any kid who wasn't a teenager yet would be on the ship. Again, here's Marion.
<v Miki Meek>孩子们整个夏天都在“伊丽莎白号”上玩耍，有时甚至要值24小时的班。如果你有十二个孩子，想想这是多么天才的育儿方式。夏天来了，你想让他们忙起来，参与其中。在任何一个夏天，基本上任何还没到十几岁的孩子都会在船上。再强调一次，这是玛丽恩说的。

00:06:44.840 --> 00:07:21.100
<v Marion>We didn't have many toys. I mean, they were-- at the end of it all, there were 12 of us. But like, then in our backyard, we had the most elaborate and amazing toy any child could ever have, that wasn't just something-- you know, it wasn't like a video game you played for a little bit. It was like years and years and years of playing on, kind of learning on, sleeping outside. It was just such a crazy, like amazing, ya know, experience.
<v Marion>我们没有很多玩具。我的意思是，到最后，我们有12个孩子。但在我们的后院，我们拥有任何孩子梦寐以求的最精致、最神奇的玩具，这不仅仅是——你知道，它不像那种玩一会儿的电子游戏。这是我们玩了多年、在上面学习、在外面睡觉的地方。这真是一种疯狂、神奇的经历。

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:40.490
<v Miki Meek>There was a whole world their dad created around the USS Elizabeth City -- a history and a mission statement, and a strict set of rules and responsibilities. His wife Joan typed up a pamphlet just like the ones the Navy handed out when their ships were at port. In this case, the port was their neighborhood, Park Ridge. Joan read it for me.
<v Miki Meek>他们的父亲在“伊丽莎白号”周围创造了一个完整的世界——一段历史、一份使命宣言，以及一套严格的规则和责任。他的妻子琼打印了一份小册子，就像海军在船只靠港时分发的那种。在这种情况下，港口就是他们所在的帕克里奇社区。琼为我读了那本手册。

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:56.900
<v Joan>"The USS Elizabeth City is one of the most modern classes of driveway gunboats. Many of our officers and enlisted personnel make their homes in this community. Our ship's mission is to defend Park Ridge's strategic Continental Divide against surprise naval attack."
<v Joan>“‘伊丽莎白号’是车道炮艇中最新型的级别之一。我们的许多军官和士兵都在这个社区安家。我们船的使命是保卫帕克里奇的战略大陆分水岭，防止突发的海军袭击。”

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:59.500
<v Miki Meek>Is that what you guys told the kids? This is your mission?
<v Miki Meek>这就是你们告诉孩子们的内容吗？这就是你们的使命？

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:06.790
<v Joan>Right. Right, definitely, yes. And of course, Park Ridge is totally surrounded by land.
<v Joan>没错。是的，绝对是。当然，帕克里奇完全被陆地包围了。

00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:10.400
<v Miki Meek>The last paragraph was a message for the crew, his 12 kids.
<v Miki Meek>最后一段是给船员们，也就是他那12个孩子的信息。

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:22.290
<v Joan>" Elizabeth City is a small ship and we can get along best by looking out for each other. Glad to have you with us. We think you're going to like it here. Welcome aboard."
<v Joan>“‘伊丽莎白号’是一艘小船，我们互相照顾才能相处得最好。很高兴你们加入我们。我们认为你们会喜欢这里的。欢迎登船。”

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:36.650
<v Miki Meek>Their dad, Jim, had been in the Navy for about a decade. In his 30s, he married Joan. They eventually ran an architecture firm together out of their basement. They didn't make much money. They spent most of their time designing youth centers in low-income neighborhoods throughout Chicago.
<v Miki Meek>他们的父亲吉姆在海军服役了大约十年。他30多岁时娶了琼。他们最终在自家地下室经营一家建筑事务所。他们没赚多少钱。他们大部分时间都在为芝加哥低收入社区设计青少年中心。

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:51.260
<v Miki Meek>But Jim didn't just design these. For more than 40 years, he also helped maintain them, would deal with emergency calls in the middle of the night if a pipe broke. And when he'd go to make repairs, he'd bring the kids to help. He was idealistic, and also incredibly practical.
<v Miki Meek>但吉姆不仅仅是设计这些。四十多年来，他一直负责维护它们，如果在半夜管道破裂，他还会处理紧急电话。当他去修理时，他会带上孩子们帮忙。他很有理想，也极其务实。

00:08:51.260 --> 00:09:06.320
<v Miki Meek>He died in 2011 and never talked to his wife and kids about what he'd hoped they'd get out of the ship. But they do know that he enjoyed playing in that world just as much as they did. Every kid I spoke to described his incredible imagination-- not just with the ship.
<v Miki Meek>他于2011年去世，从未与妻子和孩子们谈论过他希望他们从这艘船中得到什么。但他们确实知道，他像他们一样享受在那个虚构世界里玩耍的过程。我采访的每个孩子都描述了他惊人的想象力——不仅仅是在船上。

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:20.330
<v Miki Meek>There were the stories he'd make up, the Fisher Price city he built in their basement, a trading post he ran from a window in his office. The kids could get trinkets in exchange for trash they cleaned out of their yard. He printed fake money once, and they played the stock market for three months.
<v Miki Meek>他编造的故事，他在地下室建造的费雪牌城市，他在办公室窗户经营的贸易站。孩子们可以用他们从院子里清理出来的垃圾换取小玩意儿。他曾印刷过假币，他们还玩了三个月的股市。

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:31.240
<v Miki Meek>He made stuff fun. They all talked about that. But he also had an intense work ethic and thrived on discipline and teamwork. He wanted his kids to be productive and constantly told them--
<v Miki Meek>他让一切变得有趣。他们都提到了这一点。但他也有极强的工作道德，热衷于纪律和团队合作。他希望孩子们多产，并不断告诉他们——

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:36.300
<v Marion>If you have time to lean, you have time to clean. He wanted you to be working.
<v Marion>如果你有时间靠着休息，你就有时间去打扫。他希望你一直工作。

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:46.370
<v Miki Meek>Again, this is Marion. She told me her dad assigned each of them a specific job. For example, she was the hospital corpsman, which meant she was responsible for any injuries on the ship.
<v Miki Meek>还是玛丽恩。她说她父亲给他们每个人分配了特定的工作。例如，她是医院医护兵，这意味着她负责船上的任何受伤情况。

00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:59.030
<v Marion>I had like a real first aid kit. I had like a little station there. I mean, it was full of, like, my fake shots and stuff. But I had like real alcohol, real Band-Aids, real ACE bandage, you know, stuff like that.
<v Marion>我有一个真正的急救箱。我在那里有一个小小的岗位。我的意思是，它装满了我的假针头之类的东西。但我有真正的酒精、真正的创可贴、真正的弹性绷带，你知道，诸如此类的东西。

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:00.990
<v Miki Meek>What's the worst injury you had to treat?
<v Miki Meek>你处理过的最严重的伤是什么？

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:08.700
<v Marion>Like people always got splinters because it was wood. And all my siblings had a service record, or--
<v Marion>人们总是因为木头而长刺。而且我所有的兄弟姐妹都有服役记录，或者——

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:10.530
<v Miki Meek>Is it a medical record, or what?
<v Miki Meek>是医疗记录吗，还是什么？

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:35.410
<v Marion>Real medical records. And that was another job of mine, is that whenever the kids went to real medical appointments with the doctor, my mom would come back with, you know, that you get a sheet from the doctor or whatever if you had a shot and stuff. And my mom would give them, to me, and my job was to hole-punch them and to put them in their proper service jacket. And once in a while, my dad would say, will you check and see if anyone's behind on their appointments or whatever?
<v Marion>真正的医疗记录。这也是我的工作之一，每当孩子们去医生那里看病时，我妈妈回来后，你知道，如果你打了针之类的，你会从医生那里得到一张单子。我妈妈会把它们交给我，我的工作是用打孔机打孔，然后把它们放进他们各自的服役档案里。偶尔，我爸爸会说，你能检查一下看看有没有人错过了预约或者别的什么吗？

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:46.600
<v Marion>And you know, I mean, I took that very seriously. Like I was very proud that I had that job. I had this responsibility to make sure all the kids were going to the doctor on time.
<v Marion>而且你知道，我非常认真地对待这件事。我为自己有这份工作感到非常自豪。我有责任确保所有的孩子都按时去看医生。

00:10:46.600 --> 00:11:07.970
<v Miki Meek>Other kids on the ship had positions like gunner's mate, radio men, master-at-arms, and culinary specialist. And each of these involve some paperwork, real US Navy paperwork, forms that their dad saved from his time in the service or picked up from local recruitment offices. Elizabeth, who was the chief storekeeper, says she spent many hours putting her signature on supply requisitions.
<v Miki Meek>船上的其他孩子担任诸如炮手长、无线电员、军士长和烹饪专家等职位。每个人都涉及到一些文书工作，真正的美国海军文书工作，这些表格是他们父亲从服役期间保留的，或者是从当地征兵办公室拿来的。伊丽莎白是首席仓库管理员，她说她花了很多时间在补给申请单上签字。

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:18.140
<v Elizabeth>If someone was supposed to be doing some sort of job and they needed specific tools, or they were supposed to cook something and needed oatmeal or coriander--
<v Elizabeth>如果有人应该做某种工作，他们需要特定的工具，或者他们应该做点什么吃的，需要燕麦或香菜——

00:11:18.140 --> 00:11:18.830
<v Miki Meek>Or coriander?
<v Miki Meek>或者香菜？

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:20.130
<v Elizabeth>In the kitchen-- yeah. Yeah.
<v Elizabeth>在厨房里——是的。是的。

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:21.800
<v Elizabeth>[BOTH LAUGH]
<v Elizabeth>[两人笑]

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:30.640
<v Elizabeth>They would have to come to me, maybe with a work order. It was a lot of-- lot of initials. It was just more about routine and paperwork.
<v Elizabeth>他们得来找我，可能带着工作申请单。有很多很多的缩写。这更多是关于例行公事和文书工作。

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:36.030
<v Miki Meek>So what made this fun? I think that's the thing I'm trying to figure out. Because it sounds like it's a lot of work.
<v Miki Meek>那是什么让这变得有趣呢？我想这就是我试图弄明白的事情。因为听起来这需要做很多工作。

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:53.590
<v Elizabeth>Um, I don't know. I don't know that it always was fun. You know, when we were on there, my memory is it was somewhere between work and play. I think I liked that I had a defined character and everything. Grumpy old storekeeper, and you know.
<v Elizabeth>嗯，我不知道。我不确定它是否总是那么有趣。你知道，当我们在船上时，我的记忆是它介于工作和玩耍之间。我想我喜欢我有明确的角色设定。脾气暴躁的老仓库管理员，你知道。

00:11:53.590 --> 00:11:54.840
<v Miki Meek>So did your dad coach you?
<v Miki Meek>那你父亲会指导你吗？

00:11:54.840 --> 00:12:07.030
<v Elizabeth>Of course. Of course. It's just like a little snarly, and not enough coffee. No, I'm not going to get you that right now. You're going to have to wait, or fill out the right paperwork. Or you know, I don't know.
<v Elizabeth>当然。当然。就像有点咆哮，而且咖啡不够。不，我现在不会给你那个。你得等一等，或者填好正确的表格。或者你知道，我不知道。

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:30.090
<v Elizabeth>Just because that's what my father, he just remembered that every chief storekeeper that he had ever known had always had this, like, little stub of a cigar hanging out of the corner of his mouth, as he snarled at you about something. And so he felt that it was very important that I always had this little ashtray on my desk with just a smoldering little cigar on it.
<v Elizabeth>只是因为我父亲记得他认识的每一位首席仓库管理员在对你咆哮某事时，嘴角总是叼着一小截雪茄。所以他觉得我办公桌上总是放着一个装着冒烟雪茄的小烟灰缸非常重要。

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:37.790
<v Miki Meek>She was six.
<v Miki Meek>她当时六岁。

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:54.730
<v Miki Meek>Of course, with so many kids, there were arguments and disagreements on the ship. And so to deal with that, their dad created a chain of command, an entire rank structure. He gave himself one of the lower positions, the boatswain's mate. That way the kids would have to work out problems on their own.
<v Miki Meek>当然，孩子那么多，船上肯定会有争吵和分歧。为了处理这些问题，他们父亲建立了一个指挥链，一个完整的等级结构。他给自己安排了一个较低的职位，水手长。这样孩子们就不得不自己解决问题。

00:12:54.730 --> 00:13:06.370
<v Miki Meek>The highest-ranking position, commanding officer, that went to his oldest child, Jane. She was in charge of posting a plan of the day. That included chores, meals, and watch times for her brothers and sisters.
<v Miki Meek>最高等级的指挥官职位给了他最大的孩子简。她负责发布当天的日程安排。包括家务、用餐和兄弟姐妹的值班时间。

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:21.810
<v Miki Meek>If they all did their jobs, their dad would take them for doughnuts at the end of the week. Any kid who slacked off would ruin it for everyone. And if Jane caught them slacking off at their jobs, as commanding officer she had the power to punish. Like she could make you guard garbage cans as a time-out.
<v Miki Meek>如果他们都完成了工作，周末父亲会带他们去买甜甜圈。任何偷懒的孩子都会毁了大家的福利。如果简发现他们偷懒，作为指挥官，她有权惩罚他们。比如她可以罚你去看守垃圾桶作为禁闭。

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:31.160
<v Jane>I was seen as bossy and as always telling them what to do. And, you know, it didn't matter that that was my job, that that was what I had been assigned to do.
<v Jane>我被视为爱管闲事，总是指挥他们。而且，你知道，我被指派做这项工作，这是我的职责，这并不重要。

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:33.390
<v Miki Meek>So like, let me give you an example of that.
<v Miki Meek>那，让我给你举个例子。

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:46.910
<v Jane>If I was in charge and I didn't think your brass belt buckle was shined adequately, that might be a source of tension. But if you're going to wear a uniform, then you better-- you better wear it.
<v Jane>如果我负责，而我认为你的铜皮带扣擦得不够亮，那可能会引起紧张。但如果你要穿制服，那你最好——你最好把它穿好。

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:51.470
<v Miki Meek>So I mean, like in some way, it created a little bit of a separation between you and the rest of them.
<v Miki Meek>所以我的意思是，在某种程度上，这在你和其他人之间造成了一点隔阂。

00:13:51.470 --> 00:14:03.080
<v Jane>I mean, of course. It's only-- it's only natural. I mean, but what's which actually kind of funny is that I was never allowed, not one single time, to play, because that was fraternization.
<v Jane>我的意思是，当然。这是很自然的。但实际上很有趣的是，我从来没有被允许，一次都没有，去玩，因为那是“搞派系”。

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:03.870
<v Miki Meek>What?
<v Miki Meek>什么？

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:11.620
<v Jane>I was an officer. They were enlisted. I was not allowed to have that relationship with them, that I could sit around the table with them and play cards.
<v Jane>我是军官。他们是士兵。我不被允许和他们有那种关系，我不能和他们围坐在桌边打牌。

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:13.460
<v Miki Meek>When you came back in the house, were you allowed to play together?
<v Miki Meek>当你回到屋里时，你们被允许一起玩吗？

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:24.630
<v Jane>Yeah. Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah. Yeah. No, it was just-- it was just on the ship. You know, that was kind of the cost I paid for the role that I had.
<v Jane>可以。哦，当然可以。不，只是——只是在船上不可以。你知道，这是我为我的角色所付出的代价。

00:14:24.630 --> 00:14:30.510
<v Miki Meek>I asked them all, why did they keep playing along with this? So many rules. So much cleaning.
<v Miki Meek>我问他们所有人，为什么你们会一直配合这个游戏？这么多规矩。这么多清理工作。

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:49.260
<v Miki Meek>They said, you're a little kid, so it is fun. Like a treehouse and a fort and dress-up, all rolled into one. And they said when you're a little kid, you want to do with the older kids are doing, whatever it is. And then you had your dad there, always adding these extra details to keep them excited, like sudden deployment orders.
<v Miki Meek>他们说，你是个小孩子，所以这很有趣。就像树屋、堡垒和变装游戏结合在一起。他们说当你还是个孩子时，你总是想做大孩子做的事情，无论那是什么。而且你有父亲在场，总是增加这些额外的细节来让他们感到兴奋，比如突然的部署命令。

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:56.810
<v Miki Meek>The USS Elizabeth City was on wheels and went on trips. Joe remembers being called on a mission to a church parking lot down the street.
<v Miki Meek>“伊丽莎白号”是有轮子的，还会去旅行。乔记得曾接到任务前往街下的一家教堂停车场。

00:14:56.810 --> 00:15:15.560
<v Joe>And we would push this 24-foot-long ship out into a relatively busy street, Cumberland Avenue, and we would make corners. And one of us would be in the wheelhouse, actually steering the wheel of the ship. And we'd do drills. We'd do target practice, if you will, with a little submarine.
<v Joe>我们会把这艘24英尺长的船推到一条相对繁忙的街道，坎伯兰大道上，我们要转弯。我们中的一个人会坐在驾驶室里，实际操控船舵。我们会进行演习。我们会用潜艇进行目标练习。

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:36.190
<v Joe>This is back in the '80s. The Cold War was a threat. So we'd have a little Soviet-era submarine that would be out there, moving around. And we'd have "man overboard" drills, and everyone would throw on their life preservers. And I mean, even though I can clearly see asphalt sitting there, somewhere in my mind, you know, it seemed like it was real.
<v Joe>这是80年代。冷战是个威胁。所以我们有一个小型的苏联时代潜艇在那儿，四处移动。我们还会进行“人员落水”演习，每个人都会穿上救生衣。我的意思是，尽管我可以清楚地看到脚下的柏油路，但在我的脑海里，你知道，它看起来好像是真的。

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:49.060
<v Miki Meek>They lived so fully in the world of the ship, their lives were so devoted to it, that the kids never really stopped to think about how someone from the outside world might view it. Marion says she was eight before she first invited a friend aboard the USS Elizabeth City .
<v Miki Meek>他们如此沉浸在船的世界里，他们的生活如此投入，以至于孩子们从来没有想过外人会怎么看。玛丽恩说她八岁时第一次邀请朋友上“伊丽莎白号”。

00:15:49.060 --> 00:16:07.210
<v Marion>I was out there with my other brothers and sisters, and she just kept kind of rolling her eyes, being like, what are we doing? And she wanted to go inside and play Barbies, and this was kind of like weird. I think that was when I first realized, like, not everyone had ships in their backyards.
<v Marion>我和我的兄弟姐妹在外面，她只是不停地翻白眼，好像在说，我们在干什么？她想进屋去玩芭比娃娃，这感觉有点怪。我想那是第一次我意识到，并不是每个人家里后院都有船。

00:16:07.210 --> 00:16:10.770
<v Miki Meek>And so I mean, did you just not acknowledge that you had a ship in your backyard after that?
<v Miki Meek>所以我的意思是，在那之后你就假装你家后院没船了吗？

00:16:10.770 --> 00:16:16.210
<v Marion>Yeah, I think for the rest of grammar school, I just-- it was like, they just need to know. Its on a need-to-know basis.
<v Marion>是的，我想在小学剩下的时间里，我只是——就像，他们只需要知道。这是基于“需要知道”的原则。

00:16:16.210 --> 00:16:19.340
<v Marion>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Marion>[音乐播放]

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:33.530
<v Miki Meek>All the Steinfels kids, they eventually reached an age-- usually around middle school-- when they started wanting to spend more time off the ship than on it. And their dad, he created a system to deal with that, too. Here's David, the 11th of the Steinfels' 12 kids.
<v Miki Meek>斯坦费尔斯所有的孩子最终都到了一个年龄——通常在初中左右——他们开始想要在船外花更多的时间。他们的父亲也建立了一套系统来处理这个问题。这是大卫，斯坦费尔斯家12个孩子里的第11个。

00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:53.690
<v David>If you wanted to take liberty, you'd have to fill out a yellow slip and then put where you were going. You had to put the time and date of when you were leaving and when you're coming back. And then you had to put who you're going to see, what you're doing, a number to reach you at, like, your Social Security number. And then you'd have to get it signed off by the supervisor of the day. And then--
<v David>如果你想请假，你必须填一张黄色申请条，然后写上你要去哪里。你必须填上离开和回来的时间和日期。然后你必须填上你要去见谁，做什么，联系电话，比如你的社会安全号码。然后你必须让当天的监督员签字。然后——

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:54.920
<v Miki Meek>So you mean an older sibling, or what?
<v Miki Meek>所以你是说让大一点的兄弟姐妹签字，或者什么的？

00:16:54.920 --> 00:17:11.270
<v David>So the supervisor would be whoever was appointed. Then it would get posted. And I don't think my father really ever had to step in, or-- I think that's one of the beauties of the system, is like, use the other siblings to keep everything in order.
<v David>监督员就是被任命的那个人。然后它会被张贴出来。我不觉得我父亲真的需要干预，或者——我认为这是这套系统的美妙之处，就是利用其他兄弟姐妹来保持一切秩序。

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:19.780
<v Miki Meek>As the kids got older, they just stopped showing up on the ship to play. Only one kid I spoke with formally requested a discharge. She was 12.
<v Miki Meek>随着孩子们长大，他们就不再出现在船上玩了。我采访的孩子中只有一个正式要求退役。她当时12岁。

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:28.140
<v Marion>I went to my dad and, you know, acting very seriously, told him that I'd thought about it, and I had decided that it just wasn't for me anymore.
<v Marion>我去找我父亲，你知道，表现得非常严肃，告诉他我已经考虑过了，我决定这不再适合我了。

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:29.250
<v Miki Meek>This is Marion.
<v Miki Meek>这是玛丽恩。

00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:58.470
<v Marion>He was just like, OK, that's interesting. And I remember getting in the mail, not too long after that, a big manila envelope, you know, to Hospital Corpsman Third Class Marion Kate. And inside, there was a letter that said, unfortunately, because we had just entered the first Gulf War, that the President had said that no one can leave right now, that everyone's automatically re-enlisted.
<v Marion>他只是说，好吧，那很有意思。我记得在那之后不久，我在信箱里收到一个大的牛皮纸信封，你知道，写给三级医院医护兵玛丽恩·凯特。里面有一封信，说很不幸，因为我们刚刚进入第一次海湾战争，总统说现在没有人可以离开，每个人都被自动重新征召入伍。

00:17:58.470 --> 00:18:07.180
<v Miki Meek>This was an actual real order that came down for actual sailors during Operation Desert Storm. Probably not intended for middle school kids in Illinois.
<v Miki Meek>这是沙漠风暴行动期间对真正的水兵发布的真实命令。可能并不是针对伊利诺伊州的中学生。

00:18:07.180 --> 00:18:19.470
<v Marion>And so I wasn't going to be able to leave then. And I mean, I was just like, you know, kind of like, OK, he got me, I guess. I mean, there's just no getting out of it.
<v Marion>所以我当时没法离开了。我的意思是，我就像，你知道，有点像是，好吧，他逮住我了，我想。我的意思是，根本没法逃脱。

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:25.850
<v Marion>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Marion>[音乐播放]

00:18:25.850 --> 00:18:36.270
<v Miki Meek>Jane, the oldest, is 41 now. The youngest, Samuel, is 20. And you can't help but notice how much the Steinfels kids' lives seem to be shaped by their time on the ship.
<v Miki Meek>简，最大的孩子，现在41岁了。最小的塞缪尔，20岁。你不能不注意到斯坦费尔斯的孩子们的生活似乎在多大程度上受到了船上时光的影响。

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:50.720
<v Miki Meek>Elizabeth, the master chief storekeeper, she now runs her own clothing store. John, the culinary specialist, he manages the kitchen at a Chicago restaurant. Samuel's training to be an officer at the Naval Academy right now. Jane went there, too.
<v Miki Meek>伊丽莎白，首席仓库管理员，现在经营着自己的服装店。约翰，烹饪专家，管理着芝加哥一家餐厅的厨房。塞缪尔现在正在海军学院接受军官培训。简也去过那里。

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:58.250
<v Miki Meek>Four of the other boys, they ended up in the Marine Corps. David enlisted right after high school. He's been deployed to Asia and Yemen.
<v Miki Meek>其他四个男孩最终都进了海军陆战队。大卫高中毕业后就参军了。他被派驻到亚洲和也门。

00:18:58.250 --> 00:19:30.920
<v David>I think the ship was a very big part. Because the one thing that I was good at, and I knew it because we did it as a child, was the military. And when I went there, it was pretty easy for me. I mean, all these other people were struggling, especially Marine boot camp. It's a-- it's a legendary zone of horror and pain and whatnot, but I was pretty used to it. I mean, it wasn't obviously as intense at home as it was there. But a lot of the rules and everything, like organization, attention to detail, it was all pretty much mirrored.
<v David>我认为船是一个非常大的部分。因为我擅长的一件事，而且我知道这一点，是因为我们小时候这样做过，那就是军事。当我到了那里，对我来说很容易。我的意思是，所有其他人在挣扎，尤其是海军陆战队新兵训练营。那是一个——这是一个充满恐惧和痛苦的传奇地带，但我已经习惯了。我的意思是，这显然不像家里那么激烈。但很多规则和一切，比如组织能力、对细节的关注，几乎都是一样的。

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:36.210
<v Miki Meek>When you actually got on a real ship, how similar was it to your play ship?
<v Miki Meek>当你真正踏上一艘真正的船时，它和你的游戏船有多相似？

00:19:36.210 --> 00:19:46.230
<v David>[CHUCKLES] A lot of it was surprisingly the same. I mean, I could still, from my bed, reach out and touch three different people. And you could hear people breathing in their beds.
<v David>[笑声] 很多地方出奇地一样。我的意思是，我仍然可以在床上伸手触碰到三个不同的人。而且你可以听到人们在床上呼吸的声音。

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:50.600
<v David>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v David>[音乐播放]

00:19:50.600 --> 00:20:08.550
<v Miki Meek>Jim Steinfels was 71 when he died from cancer four years ago. He wanted his kids to be competent and industrious and serve something bigger than themselves, lessons they say they learned on the ship. Right after he died, Marion said they couldn't stop thinking about how he would have hated anyone wasting a good day of work.
<v Miki Meek>吉姆·斯坦费尔斯四年前因癌症去世时71岁。他希望他的孩子们能干、勤奋，并为比他们自身更伟大的事业服务，这是他们说在船上学到的教训。在他去世后，玛丽恩说他们忍不住一直在想，他会多么痛恨有人浪费美好的一天去工作。

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:19.420
<v Marion>My mom, one thing that she kind of struggled with was having his funeral during the day. She thought that he would be mortified that people had stopped working to go to his funeral. So she thought about having it in the evening.
<v Marion>我妈，有一件事她挺挣扎的，就是要在白天举行他的葬礼。她认为他会因为人们停止工作去参加他的葬礼而感到羞愧。所以她想在晚上举行。

00:20:19.420 --> 00:20:29.150
<v Marion>And my dad actually built his own coffin. I mean, he always made-- you know, I mean, he made the ship. He was really good at making things.
<v Marion>我爸爸其实自己造了他的棺材。我的意思是，他总是做——你知道，我的意思是，他造了那艘船。他真的很擅长制作东西。

00:20:29.150 --> 00:20:47.730
<v Marion>I just miss being on the ship. I mean, and I think that we all wouldn't have the relationships we have. I mean, I really think it was kind of the basis for our closeness and our kind of like camaraderie.
<v Marion>我只是很怀念在船上的日子。我的意思是，我认为我们都不会有现在的关系。我的意思是，我真的认为那是我们亲密关系和友谊的基础。

00:20:47.730 --> 00:21:05.330
<v Miki Meek>The ship their dad build is still around. It's in the garage at their mom's house. And every once in a while, when the kids are home visiting, they'll pull it back into their yard. Sometimes they'll climb back on it, though it's tough to get through the portholes and doors now. And then they'll stand on the deck and watch their own children play on it.
<v Miki Meek>他们父亲造的那艘船还在。它在他们妈妈家的车库里。偶尔，当孩子们回家探亲时，他们会把它拉回院子里。有时他们会爬回去，虽然现在要穿过舷窗和门有点困难了。然后他们会站在甲板上，看着自己的孩子在上面玩耍。

