<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们都会选择一个主题，为您带来关于该主题的不同故事。

00:22:25.540 --> 00:22:38.840
<v Ira Glass>Today's show, "Land of Make-Believe," we have stories of people constructing worlds that are not real, some more convincingly and charmingly than others. We've arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, The Lyin' Kings.
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目，“虚构的土地”，我们有关于人们构建非真实世界的故事，有些人比其他人更令人信服、更有魅力。我们已经进入节目的第二幕。第二幕，说谎的国王。

00:22:38.840 --> 00:22:54.390
<v Ira Glass>So this next story is about a whole bunch of people who are building an elaborate world of make-believe for their jobs, and they fail. They totally fail. This is not a spoiler. They don't come close to getting this right. I'm telling you up front. Jonathan Menjivar explains what happened.
<v Ira Glass>所以接下来的这个故事是关于一大群人，他们为了工作建立了一个复杂的虚构世界，但他们失败了。他们彻底失败了。这不是剧透。他们根本没能把这件事做好。我提前告诉你。乔纳森·门吉瓦尔解释了发生了什么。

00:22:54.390 --> 00:23:10.610
<v Ira Glass>A warning, if you're listening to the podcast or online version of our program-- there's cursing in the story that we beeped out on the radio. But on the internet we have unbeeped it. If you're listening with kids or you just prefer a beeped show, as always, you can find that at our website. OK, here's Jonathan.
<v Ira Glass>一个警告，如果你正在听播客或在线版本的节目——故事中有一些我们在广播中消音的咒骂。但在互联网上我们没有消音。如果你和孩子一起听，或者你更喜欢消音版节目，像往常一样，你可以在我们的网站上找到它。好的，这是乔纳森。

00:23:10.610 --> 00:23:22.360
<v Jonathan Menjivar>This story starts with a lie between friends. One of those friends was a young woman named Lyric R. Cabral. She lived in Harlem and had this neighbor, a guy in his 50s named Saeed Torres.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>这个故事始于朋友之间的谎言。其中一个朋友是一位名叫莉莉克·R·卡布拉尔的年轻女性。她住在哈莱姆区，有一个邻居，一个50多岁的男人，名叫赛义德·托雷斯。

00:23:22.360 --> 00:23:30.280
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Lyric's apartment was on the third floor of a brownstone. Saeed lived on the first. And they hung out together all the time. They're both black and Muslim.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>莉莉克的公寓在褐砂石建筑的三楼。赛义德住在二楼。他们经常在一起玩。他们都是黑人，也是穆斯林。

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:44.010
<v Jonathan Menjivar>And Lyric found Saeed intriguing, in part because he was an actual Black Panther. Lyric had grown up interested in the Black Panther Party but had never actually met a member before. She was a student at the time, learning to do documentary work.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>莉莉克觉得赛义德很有趣，部分原因是他确实曾是黑豹党成员。莉莉克从小就对黑豹党感兴趣，但从未真正见过成员。她当时是一名学生，正在学习纪录片制作。

00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:49.770
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Saeed had told Lyric that he was going to be moving to the Bronx soon. And then one day she came home from school.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>赛义德告诉莉莉克他很快要搬到布朗克斯区。然后有一天她从学校回来。

00:23:49.770 --> 00:24:03.080
<v Lyric R. Cabral>I usually stopped in his apartment to say hello, just because it was before I went upstairs, I would pass his apartment. And when I looked in the apartment, all of his things were gone. There was no furniture. There was no indication that anyone had ever lived there.
<v Lyric R. Cabral>我通常会去他公寓打个招呼，因为在我上楼之前我会经过他的公寓。当我往公寓里看时，他所有的东西都不见了。没有家具。没有任何迹象表明有人曾经住过那里。

00:24:03.080 --> 00:24:09.770
<v Lyric R. Cabral>And I assumed that he had probably just moved to the Bronx. But I thought it weird, because I was like, you know, it was too hasty. He would have told me.
<v Lyric R. Cabral>我以为他可能只是搬到了布朗克斯区。但我觉得很奇怪，因为我想，你知道，这太仓促了。他应该会告诉我的。

00:24:09.770 --> 00:24:21.280
<v Lyric R. Cabral>And while I'm staring at the empty apartment, I get a call from him. He sounds incredibly frantic. I had never heard that tone in his voice. He sounded like almost someone was on the other end of the phone with a gun to his head.
<v Lyric R. Cabral>就在我盯着空荡荡的公寓时，接到了他的电话。他听起来极其疯狂。我从未听过他那样的语气。他听起来就像电话那头有人拿着枪指着他的头。

00:24:21.280 --> 00:24:28.630
<v Lyric R. Cabral>And he said, if anyone comes looking for me, if anyone asks you any information about me, don't give it to them. And you find out who they are for me.
<v Lyric R. Cabral>他说，如果有人来找我，如果有人向你询问关于我的任何信息，不要告诉他们。你要帮我找出他们是谁。

00:24:28.630 --> 00:24:35.880
<v Lyric R. Cabral>And I said, well-- you know, I was pretty petrified based on the way he sounded. He sounded incredibly nervous. And so I said, well, where are you? What's wrong?
<v Lyric R. Cabral>我说，嗯——你知道，基于他的语气，我非常害怕。他听起来极其紧张。所以我说，那你现在在哪？出什么事了？

00:24:35.880 --> 00:24:42.410
<v Lyric R. Cabral>And he's like, well, I can't really tell you, but I'm in South Carolina right now. I have something to tell you. You should come down here and visit.
<v Lyric R. Cabral>他说，我没法告诉你，但我现在在南卡罗来纳州。我有些话要告诉你。你应该过来看看。

00:24:42.410 --> 00:24:57.490
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Which she did. And Saeed told her that all these years she'd known him, all these years she'd seen him getting dressed in nice suits and going to work, that was all a cover. Because Saeed was actually an informant for the FBI.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>她确实去了。赛义德告诉她，这些年她认识他，这些年她看着他穿着漂亮的西装去上班，那都是伪装。因为赛义德实际上是FBI的线人。

00:24:57.490 --> 00:25:01.460
<v Jonathan Menjivar>She says, at the time, she didn't know much about how FBI investigations work.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>她说，当时，她对FBI的调查是如何运作的了解不多。

00:25:01.460 --> 00:25:15.600
<v Lyric R. Cabral>So it was a very naive back-and-forth, but it was very braggadocious on his part. He outlined all that he had done, sort of, for the FBI. And when I asked him, why are you telling me this? He goes, well, because you don't have to fact-check me. You were there.
<v Lyric R. Cabral>所以那是一次非常天真的交流，但他非常自夸。他概述了他为FBI所做的一切。当我问他，你为什么要告诉我这些？他说，嗯，因为你不需要去核实我。你当时就在现场。

00:25:15.600 --> 00:25:35.130
<v Jonathan Menjivar>"There" in that first floor apartment in Harlem. Saeed told Lyric that the FBI had paid his rent while he investigated a man named Tariq Shah. And Lyric knew exactly who that was, because Saeed had introduced her to Tariq. He'd come to Saeed's apartment several times when she was there. Saeed also told Lyric that his whole apartment was wired.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>在哈莱姆区一楼的那个公寓里。赛义德告诉莉莉克，FBI支付了他的租金，而他正在调查一个名叫塔里克·沙阿的人。莉莉克很清楚那个人是谁，因为赛义德把她介绍给了塔里克。他曾在她在场时去过赛义德的公寓好几次。赛义德还告诉莉莉克，他的整个公寓都被安装了窃听器。

00:25:35.130 --> 00:25:46.280
<v Lyric R. Cabral>I was pissed. You know, I mean definitely, this was not like-- I didn't receive this information with a smile. I definitely felt betrayed. Because to me, that meant that I had an FBI file.
<v Lyric R. Cabral>我很生气。你知道，我的意思是绝对的，这并不是像——我并没有微笑着接受这些信息。我绝对感到被背叛了。因为对我来说，这意味着我有一份FBI档案。

00:25:46.280 --> 00:26:01.810
<v Lyric R. Cabral>I mean, and that was a question I had for him. I was like, well, since I was in the apartment with Tariq, I was on camera, correct? And to date, I have not gotten a good answer, both from the FBI-- sort of FOIA'd that material. That FBI has told me they do not have anything in their possession.
<v Lyric R. Cabral>我的意思是，这是一个我问他的问题。我说，既然我和塔里克在同一个公寓里，我被摄像机拍到了，对吧？迄今为止，我没有从FBI那里得到一个好的答案——我通过信息自由法案调取了那些材料。FBI告诉我，他们手头没有任何关于我的记录。

00:26:01.810 --> 00:26:11.400
<v Lyric R. Cabral>And Saeed himself says, oh, no, I would never record you. I would never press Play when you were there. I would never press Record. But I'm not quite too confident in that.
<v Lyric R. Cabral>赛义德本人说，哦，不，我永远不会记录你。你在这里的时候我绝不会按播放键。我绝不会按录音键。但我并不太相信这一点。

00:26:11.400 --> 00:26:25.500
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Lyric says that there in South Carolina, Saeed handed her a folder full of newspaper articles about cases he'd worked on. He'd actually highlighted the parts in the articles that talked about his role, places where he's identified just as "government informant."
<v Jonathan Menjivar>莉莉克说，在南卡罗来纳州，赛义德递给她一个装满关于他曾参与案件的报纸文章的文件夹。他实际上在文章中高亮显示了讨论他角色的部分，那些他被标识为“政府线人”的地方。

00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:34.500
<v Jonathan Menjivar>And Lyric believed him, believed that he'd been an informant, that he'd fled to South Carolina because he was afraid for his safety after the FBI arrested Tariq Shah.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>莉莉克相信了他，相信他是线人，因为他在FBI逮捕塔里克·沙阿后害怕自己的安全而逃到了南卡罗来纳州。

00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:47.670
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Lyric didn't know exactly why Saeed was telling her all of this, but he mentioned he'd seen other people turning the work they'd done with the FBI into bestselling books. Why couldn't he do something similar? And that's where things stood, for nearly a decade.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>莉莉克并不完全知道赛义德为什么告诉她这些，但他提到他曾看到其他人将他们与FBI合作所做的工作变成了畅销书。为什么他不能做类似的事情呢？事情就这样持续了近十年。

00:26:47.670 --> 00:27:00.740
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Lyric kept in touch with Saeed and started pursuing a career as a documentary filmmaker. She met another filmmaker named David Felix Sutcliffe. And David told her that he'd been fantasizing about making a film that would follow an FBI informant.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>莉莉克与赛义德保持联系，并开始从事纪录片导演的工作。她遇到了另一位名叫大卫·菲利克斯·萨特克里夫的导演。大卫告诉她，他一直幻想拍一部关于跟踪FBI线人的电影。

00:27:00.740 --> 00:27:04.090
<v David Felix Sutcliffe>And she said, funny you should say, because I-- I know one.
<v David Felix Sutcliffe>她说，你这么说真巧，因为我——我认识一个。

00:27:04.090 --> 00:27:11.460
<v Jonathan Menjivar>That's David, by the way. He and Lyric reached out to Saeed, and he told them that he was just starting to work on a new case in Pittsburgh.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>顺便说一句，这就是大卫。他和莉莉克联系了赛义德，赛义德告诉他们他刚开始在匹兹堡负责一个新案子。

00:27:11.460 --> 00:27:17.930
<v David Felix Sutcliffe>And, you know, for reasons that weren't totally clear to us at the time, he said, yeah, sure, let's do this. Let's-- you guys can film me.
<v David Felix Sutcliffe>而且，你知道，出于当时我们不太清楚的原因，他说，好的，没问题。你们可以拍我。

00:27:17.930 --> 00:27:22.490
<v Saeed Torres>So what's going on? You didn't come down here with no cameras and all that. I asked you that earlier.
<v Saeed Torres>那到底是怎么回事？你们当初过来时没带摄像机什么的。我早先问过你。

00:27:22.490 --> 00:27:23.280
<v Lyric R. Cabral>No, you did not--
<v Lyric R. Cabral>不，你没有——

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:29.260
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Sure, they could film. But Saeed often got annoyed when they showed up. It was unpredictable when he'd be OK with it.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>当然，他们可以拍摄。但当他们出现时，赛义德经常感到恼火。他什么时候能接受拍摄是不可预测的。

00:27:29.260 --> 00:27:42.980
<v Saeed Torres>I said don't come down here with no goddamn cameras and stuff. Hello? I told you I didn't want my face in this shit. You'd be surprised who knows me. Everybody in Brooklyn know me.
<v Saeed Torres>我说过别带什么该死的摄像机过来。喂？我告诉过你我不想让我的脸出现在这些屁事里。你会惊讶有谁认识我。布鲁克林所有人都认识我。

00:27:42.980 --> 00:27:44.350
<v Lyric R. Cabral>But they don't know where you are.
<v Lyric R. Cabral>但他们不知道你在哪。

00:27:44.350 --> 00:27:46.310
<v Saeed Torres>And they know me in Harlem, too. They know my face.
<v Saeed Torres>哈莱姆区的人也认识我。他们认识我的脸。

00:27:46.310 --> 00:27:49.880
<v Lyric R. Cabral>But they don't know where you are.
<v Lyric R. Cabral>但他们不知道你在哪。

00:27:49.880 --> 00:27:53.920
<v Saeed Torres>I might not even make no fucking independent film, motherfuckers come after me.
<v Saeed Torres>我可能连个该死的独立电影都拍不成，那些混蛋会来找我的。

00:27:53.920 --> 00:28:01.930
<v Jonathan Menjivar>That clip is from the documentary Lyric and David ended up making. It's called (T)ERROR . It follows Saeed as he's working this investigation in Pittsburgh.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>这段剪辑来自莉莉克和大卫最终拍摄的纪录片。它叫《恐怖》(T)ERROR。它跟踪记录了赛义德在匹兹堡进行的调查。

00:28:01.930 --> 00:28:17.910
<v Jonathan Menjivar>And in case this isn't clear, I want to point out how incredibly rare this footage is. Because normally when you hear a story about an FBI counterterrorism investigation, it's pieced together after the fact, using court records and whatever interviews reporters can get with people who will talk.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>如果这还不清楚，我想指出这种镜头是多么罕见。因为通常当你听到关于FBI反恐调查的故事时，它是事后拼凑出来的，使用法庭记录和记者能从愿意交谈的人那里得到的任何采访。

00:28:17.910 --> 00:28:33.410
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Here, Lyric and David were following someone who usually doesn't talk in the first place, an FBI informant. They were following him in real time, while he was actively working a case. So they didn't have to rely on Saeed's memory to document what happened. They were there.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>在这里，莉莉克和大卫跟踪的是一个通常根本不会说话的人，一个FBI线人。他们是在实时跟踪他，当时他正积极地处理案件。所以他们不需要依赖赛义德的记忆来记录发生了什么。他们在现场。

00:28:33.410 --> 00:28:51.730
<v Jonathan Menjivar>They hung out with him in his FBI safe house. He showed them pictures he'd pinned on the wall of targets he said the FBI was interested in. As best as anyone can tell, the FBI never agreed to this. And apparently they never noticed that Lyric and David were filming all this time. I mean, why would they have let the investigation continue, if they knew?
<v Jonathan Menjivar>他们和他一起待在他的FBI安全屋里。他给他们展示了贴在墙上的照片，那些他说FBI感兴趣的目标。据所知，FBI从未同意这一点。显然他们从未注意到莉莉克和大卫一直在拍摄。我的意思是，如果他们知道，他们为什么要让调查继续下去呢？

00:28:51.730 --> 00:28:56.070
<v Jonathan Menjivar>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Jonathan Menjivar>[音乐播放]

00:28:56.070 --> 00:29:12.140
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Saeed's primary target, the POI-- Person of Interest-- in Pittsburgh was a man Khalifa Ali al-Akili. Khalifa's a white guy, a convert to Islam. Here's a video he posted to YouTube. He's got a beard, a white turban.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>赛义德在匹兹堡的主要目标，POI——相关人员——是一个名叫哈利法·阿里·阿基利的人。哈利法是一个白人，皈依了伊斯兰教。这是他在YouTube上发布的一个视频。他留着胡子，戴着白色的头巾。

