<v Jonathan Menjivar>Sharif told Lyric that he's no longer an FBI informant, that they don't respond to his calls or texts. They've gone completely silent.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>谢里夫告诉莉莉克他不再是FBI线人了，他们不再回复他的电话或短信。他们已经完全沉默了。

00:49:27.210 --> 00:49:52.810
<v Jonathan Menjivar>And Khalifa? Well, Khalifa had planned to go public with everything-- everything he'd written in that Facebook post, everything he'd told David and Lyric. And he'd been in touch with lawyers at an advocacy group called Project Salaam that provides legal help for Muslims. Khalifa was going to go down to DC and be part of a press conference where he'd lay out the whole story. The day before the press conference, David was supposed to meet him early to film him going to morning prayer.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>那哈利法呢？好吧，哈利法已经计划公开一切——他写在Facebook帖子里的每一件事，他告诉大卫和莉莉克的每一件事。他一直与一个叫“沙拉姆计划”的倡导团体的律师保持联系，该团体为穆斯林提供法律援助。哈利法打算去华盛顿参加新闻发布会，在那里他会把整个故事讲出来。新闻发布会前一天，大卫应该早点去和他见面，拍摄他去晨祷的过程。

00:49:52.810 --> 00:50:02.310
<v David Felix Sutcliffe>And I showed up outside of his house. I pull around the corner, I notice all these black SUVs out front. And you know, it's probably about 5:30 in the morning, and I knew exactly what was going on.
<v David Felix Sutcliffe>我出现在他家门外。我把车开到拐角处，注意到门前全是这些黑色SUV。你知道，大概是凌晨5:30，我知道发生了什么。

00:50:02.310 --> 00:50:04.000
<v Khalifa Al-akili>I haven't done anything to anybody.
<v Khalifa Al-akili>我没对任何人做过任何事。

00:50:04.000 --> 00:50:05.640
<v Man>--And get that squared away.
<v Man>——把那个处理好。

00:50:05.640 --> 00:50:15.460
<v Khalifa Al-akili>The Prophet Muhammad, salla ilahu, he said, "Beware of the supplication of the person who is unjustly treated." This is wrong. I haven't done anything to anyone.
<v Khalifa Al-akili>先知穆罕默德说，“要当心被不公正对待的人的祈求。”这不对。我没对任何人做过任何事。

00:50:15.460 --> 00:50:18.640
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Khalifa was in handcuffs, being arrested by the FBI.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>哈利法被戴上手铐，被FBI逮捕了。

00:50:18.640 --> 00:50:30.300
<v David Felix Sutcliffe>Khalifa had met with his lawyer a few days earlier, who had said, you know, if they're going to come for you, it's going to be either late at night or early in the morning. And so fortunately, I had the camera set up.  I just grabbed it and jumped out of the car.
<v David Felix Sutcliffe>哈利法几天前见过他的律师，律师说，你知道，如果他们要来抓你，不是在深夜就是在凌晨。所以幸运的是，我架好了摄像机。我只是抓起它跳下车。

00:50:30.300 --> 00:50:37.860
<v Khalifa Al-akili>Can you please try to get ahold of someone to help my wife, please? Please, man.
<v Khalifa Al-akili>请帮我联系人来帮帮我妻子，好吗？求你了，伙计。

00:50:37.860 --> 00:51:01.340
<v Jonathan Menjivar>The FBI arrested Khalifa not for terrorism charges, but on a gun charge. Khalifa, remember, was a felon. He'd done time for a narcotics charge. So he wasn't legally supposed to possess a firearm. But one day, years before, he went to a public firing range, borrowed a friend's gun, and shot at a target. His friend took a seven-second video and emailed it to Khalifa.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>FBI逮捕哈利法不是因为恐怖主义指控，而是因为枪支指控。记住，哈利法是重罪犯。他因毒品指控服过刑。所以他法律上不允许持有枪支。但有一天，多年前，他去了一个公共射击场，借了朋友的枪，向一个目标射击。他的朋友拍了一段七秒的视频并用电子邮件发给了哈利法。

00:51:01.340 --> 00:51:22.550
<v Lyric R. Cabral>And basically, that friend was called in to the grand jury, and basically, the friend had to admit, yes, I emailed this photo to Khalifa. Yes, this photo was taken at a shooting range, wherein Khalifa was holding a gun. And that's all the information they needed, in combination with that photo, led to confirmation that Khalifa was indeed a felon in possession of a firearm. And he received a sentence of eight years.
<v Lyric R. Cabral>基本上，那个朋友被传唤到大陪审团，基本上，那个朋友不得不承认，是的，我把这张照片发电子邮件给了哈利法。是的，这张照片是在哈利法持枪的射击场拍摄的。这就是他们需要的所有信息，结合那张照片，导致了确认哈利法确实是持有枪支的重罪犯。他被判了八年。

00:51:22.550 --> 00:51:38.110
<v David Felix Sutcliffe>And yet his public defender, who said she's handled thousands of gun cases throughout her career, she said she has never once seen someone being prosecuted based on a photograph of them at a gun range. You know, the vast majority of the time, it's someone who's caught in criminal activity.
<v David Felix Sutcliffe>然而他的公设辩护律师，她说她在职业生涯中处理过成千上万起枪支案件，她说她从来没见过有人因为一张在射击场的照片而被起诉。你知道，绝大多数情况下，那是被抓到犯罪活动的人。

00:51:38.110 --> 00:51:55.000
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Khalifa had gone to the gun range in 2010, and he hadn't tried to hide it. For a while, a picture of him holding the gun had been his Facebook profile picture. Which means the FBI could've made this arrest almost two years earlier, long before they dragged Sharif into their investigation.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>哈利法在2010年去过射击场，他没试图隐瞒。有一阵子，他持枪的照片甚至是他的Facebook头像。这意味着FBI本可以在近两年前就进行这次逮捕，早在他们把谢里夫拖入调查之前。

00:51:55.000 --> 00:52:20.690
<v Jonathan Menjivar>The FBI did not comment for Lyric and David's film. And when we approached them, they provided this statement. "The FBI's use of undercover operations and informants across all criminal and national security programs is done in accordance with strict guidelines and in close coordination with the Department of Justice and the United States Attorney's offices."
<v Jonathan Menjivar>FBI没有对莉莉克的电影置评。当我们找到他们时，他们发表了这份声明。“FBI在所有刑事和国家安全项目中对卧底行动和线人的使用，都是按照严格指导方针并在与司法部和美国检察官办公室密切协调下进行的。”

00:52:20.690 --> 00:52:31.590
<v Jonathan Menjivar>The person I really wanted to talk to about this whole experience is Sharif, see what he thinks now that he's seen Lyric and David's film and he knows the truth from all sides. And it seemed like he'd be game to talk.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>关于整个经历，我真正想谈谈的人是谢里夫，看看他在看过莉莉克和大卫的电影并了解各方真相后有什么想法。他似乎也乐意谈谈。

00:52:31.590 --> 00:52:47.720
<v Jonathan Menjivar>But at the last minute, he turned me down. Lyric says it's because I called him an informant in an email. He considers himself to be a civilian operative, Lyric told me. He knows that some people look down on informants, and he was offended that I used the word.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>但在最后一刻，他拒绝了我。莉莉克说是因为我在电子邮件里叫他线人。他认为自己是文职特工，莉莉克告诉我。他知道有些人看不起线人，而我用这个词冒犯了他。

00:52:47.720 --> 00:52:55.750
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Lyric says that when Sharif finally had a chance to watch the movie, he had one critique. "I look fucking depressed all the time," he said.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>莉莉克说，当谢里夫终于有机会看这部电影时，他提出了一个批评。“我看起来一直很沮丧，”他说。

00:52:55.750 --> 00:53:15.780
<v Jonathan Menjivar>For 23 years, Saeed Sharif Torres had been doing informant work, coming up with a new version of himself each time for each new target. He's a shell of what he once was, Lyric told me. Lying to people for a living can take a toll on you. Now he could finally see it.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>23年来，赛义德·谢里夫·托雷斯一直在做线人工作，每次为每个新目标编造一个新的自己。他已经成了他过去的一个空壳，莉莉克告诉我。以说谎为生会让你付出代价。现在他终于看到了。

00:53:15.780 --> 00:53:25.540
